---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';

export function getStaticPaths() {
	return projects.map((project) => ({
		params: { slug: project.slug },
		props: { project },
	}));
}

const { project } = Astro.props;
---

<Layout title={`${project.title} — Hiro Eernisse`} description={project.blurb}>
	<Nav />

	<main class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-12">
		{/* No back link up here on purpose. It used to be that the sticky nav's
		    Projects link never left the viewport, so a second one this close read
		    as two of the same control; that link is gone (08-01), and the reason
		    is now simply that the footer's does the job better — it returns you
		    to the card you came from, at the scroll position you left it. */}

		{/* Dateline is the year alone — the company already reads off the prose
		    below, and tags/Visit were dropped from this page (both still live in
		    projects.ts).

		    ── 2026-08-10: THIS PAGE TAKES THE ABOUT PAGE'S SCALE AND PALETTE ──
		    Hiro's ask, and it applies to the whole page, so read this note before
		    changing any size or colour below. The project page had been running
		    its own type scale (title 35.625/42.75px, lead 23.75, body 21.375) and
		    its own two accents (--ink-title on the h1, --ink-num on the year).
		    The about page runs THREE values and no accents: headings at
		    --ink-heading, prose at 16.625px in plain --ink, ancillary text at
		    --ink-soft. That set is now this page's set too.

		    --ink-soft on the year, not --ink-num. The about page has no numerals,
		    so there was no counterpart to match — what it does have is small
		    ancillary text (the photo caption, the footer) and that runs
		    --ink-soft. The year rode ON the muted line already and the orange was
		    there to lift it out of it, which is the thing that goes.
		    ⚠ This was --ink-num's LAST use on the site; the card datelines dropped
		    it on 08-02 (see ProjectCard.astro). The token is still defined in
		    global.css and nothing consumes it. Left there rather than deleted —
		    that is a palette decision, not a page one.

		    NO sm:text-4xl. The h1 sat one step larger from sm up; the about page's
		    greeting is text-3xl at every width and this now matches it, so the
		    title is 35.625px on a phone and on a desktop alike. */}
		<header class="reveal" style="--i: 0">
			<p class="text-sm tabular-nums text-[var(--ink-soft)]">{project.year}</p>
			<h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">{project.title}</h1>
		</header>

		{/* Reading matter is capped at --measure (see global.css) — ~11 words a
		    line — while the page itself spans the nav's full 960px, which the
		    images below fill.

		    No "Background" / "Work" headings: two paragraphs in one screen give
		    a reader nothing to skip to, and the labels were the same small-bold
		    kicker shape the home page dropped.

		    ⚠ THE LEAD IS GONE (2026-08-10, with the about-page scale — see the
		    header note). The register change the dropped headings used to mark
		    — context, then what I did — rode the TYPE from 08-01 on: the problem
		    paragraph was a step up in size (23.75px) and set in --ink-soft, so
		    the work paragraph read as the body it stepped down into. The about
		    page sets every paragraph identically, so matching it spends that
		    device: both are now 16.625px in plain --ink and nothing but the
		    paragraph break separates them. That is a real loss and it was the
		    price of the ask, not an oversight — if the two paragraphs ever need
		    telling apart again, the headings are the honest fix, because the
		    type has no step left to make.

		    Nothing here is capped by these numbers, but the MEASURE now is: the
		    section is still max-w-[var(--measure)] (35rem / 665px), which held
		    ~11 words a line at 21.375px and holds ~80 characters at 16.625 —
		    past the usual 45–75. The about page's own column is 511.5px and ~62
		    characters. Narrowing this is the obvious follow-up and was left
		    alone deliberately: it is a layout change, and the ask was sizes and
		    colours. The page itself still spans the nav's full 960px, which the
		    images below fill. */}
		<section class="reveal mt-8 max-w-[var(--measure)]" style="--i: 1">
			{/* Both paragraphs keep the UI weight tier (w400 dark / w500 light)
			    rather than inheriting the body's w300 — the only weight bumps the
			    2026-08-04 legibility audit actually required, and the ONE thing
			    the 08-10 about-page pass did not touch. Weight is neither a size
			    nor a colour, and this is the site's longest-form reading; dropping
			    to w300 would quietly undo an audit finding on prose that just got
			    5px smaller, which is the wrong direction. So these paragraphs
			    render at the about page's size and colour a notch heavier than its
			    own prose does. If an exact match is wanted, deleting both
			    [font-weight:…] is the whole change.

			    The audit's numbers are STALE and deliberately not restated: they
			    were measured at 23.75px/--ink-soft and 21.375px/--ink, and both
			    paragraphs are now 16.625px/--ink. Smaller type needs MORE weight
			    for the same APCA floor, so w400 no longer clears what it cleared
			    then — but the about page's prose is 16.625px at w300 in the same
			    ink and the audit already lists it as a standing contrast item
			    that weight cannot fix. This page now sits in that same accepted
			    posture rather than in one of its own. Re-measure before quoting
			    any figure here.

			    What this DID close: the lead was the site's one residual APCA
			    shortfall, at 23.75px in --ink-soft where the Lc wanted 35px and no
			    weight reached the floor. Moving it to --ink retires that entry —
			    it was a colour gap, and the colour is what changed. */}
			<p class="text-sm [font-weight:var(--weight-ui)] leading-relaxed">{project.problem}</p>
			<p class="mt-6 text-sm [font-weight:var(--weight-ui)] leading-relaxed">{project.work}</p>
		</section>

		<section class="reveal mt-10 space-y-5" style="--i: 2">
			{
				project.images.map((src, i) => (
					<img
						src={src}
						alt={`${project.title} — image ${i + 1}`}
						loading={i === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
						class="w-full rounded-[var(--radius)] border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface)]"
					/>
				))
			}
		</section>
	</main>

	{/* ── NO FOOTER (2026-08-10, Hiro: remove the back-to-projects button) ──
	    This page had one and it held exactly one thing: a "Back to projects"
	    link whose slug sent you to the CARD you came from, at the scroll
	    position you left it. It was the last navigation on the page, several
	    thousand pixels of screenshots below where you started; the way back is
	    now the browser's own Back button and the wordmark.

	    ⚠ IT WAS ALSO THE ENTRY POINT TO THE WHOLE PROJECTS-LANDING PIPELINE,
	    which is the part that isn't obvious from the markup that just left.
	    That anchor carried `data-projects-link`, and it was the ONLY element on
	    the site that did — the nav's own Projects link went on 08-01. So
	    Nav.astro's handler for `a[data-projects-link]` now matches nothing, and
	    everything downstream of it is unreachable rather than merely unused:
	      · `sessionStorage['nav-projects']` has no writer, so `projectsIntent`
	        is always null on arrival;
	      · therefore `.projects-landing`, `landOnProjects()`, `reveal-hold-land`
	        and `reveal-skip` never fire;
	      · `goToProjects()` has no caller;
	      · `nav-return` is still WRITTEN on every card click, but `returnY` is
	        only read inside an `if (projectsIntent)`, so it is now write-only.
	    None of that was deleted — the ask was the button, and unpicking a
	    cross-page scroll-restore system is its own change with its own risks.
	    Read the notes in Nav.astro before either reviving or removing it; the
	    machinery is intact and correct, it simply has nothing to start it.

	    `.btn-quiet` in global.css is likewise orphaned — this was its only
	    consumer. Same reasoning: left standing, flagged there.

	    The page now ends on the last screenshot with only main's py-12 (57px)
	    under it, where the footer added another 47.5. It is also the only route
	    with no footer — home and about both carry the copyright line. If the
	    ending reads too abrupt, that line is the drop-in, and about.astro's
	    footer note explains why this page never took it. */}
</Layout>
